WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.440
A traumatic event can change the way you think

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.140
about yourself and the world.

3
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.410
You might think you are to blame for what happened or believe

4
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.640
you don't deserve to be happy.

5
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.550
You may start to believe the world is unsafe --

6
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.120
doing things like going to a grocery store or restaurant

7
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.850
may seem too dangerous.

8
00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.950
These kinds of thoughts are common in people

9
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.990
with posttraumatic stress disorder, or PTSD.

10
00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:27.090
They're called "stuck points." They keep you stuck in your

11
00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.760
PTSD and cause you to miss out on the people,

12
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.100
places or activities you used to enjoy.

13
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.170
So how do you get "unstuck"?

14
00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.970
Cognitive Processing Therapy -- or CPT -- is a PTSD treatment

15
00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:42.980
that can help you break the negative thinking

16
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:43.940
that's holding you back.

17
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.080
It's based on the idea that our thoughts affect how

18
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.880
we feel and how we act.

19
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.720
In CPT, you and your therapist will talk about how your

20
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.620
negative thoughts about the trauma -- those "stuck points"

21
00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.020
-- have changed you: how safe and in control you feel,

22
00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.030
how much you trust yourself and others,

23
00:00:59.030 --> 00:00:61.030
and even your sense of self-worth.

24
00:00:61.030 --> 00:00:63.100
You'll practice a strategy to change -- or challenge --

25
00:00:63.100 --> 00:00:64.100
your stuck points.

26
00:00:64.100 --> 00:00:68.570
PTSD can make you jump to negative conclusions,

27
00:00:68.570 --> 00:00:70.800
but there may be other ways to think about things that are more

28
00:00:70.800 --> 00:00:73.210
accurate -- and less upsetting.

29
00:00:73.210 --> 00:00:75.440
Imagine you're driving down the highway,

30
00:00:75.440 --> 00:00:76.680
and someone swerves in front of you.

31
00:00:76.680 --> 00:00:80.680
You might blow up at the driver: "What a jerk!"

32
00:00:80.680 --> 00:00:82.850
But what if he was racing to the hospital,

33
00:00:82.850 --> 00:00:85.890
or having a panic attack and rushing to get off the road?

34
00:00:85.890 --> 00:00:87.890
If you considered these possibilities,

35
00:00:87.890 --> 00:00:89.490
you might not feel as angry.

36
00:00:89.490 --> 00:00:92.760
The bottom line: small changes in thinking can have a big

37
00:00:92.760 --> 00:00:95.090
impact on how you feel.

38
00:00:95.090 --> 00:00:97.460
Some people find that writing about their trauma helps

39
00:00:97.460 --> 00:00:99.630
them change upsetting thoughts and feelings.

40
00:00:99.630 --> 00:00:101.630
You and your therapist can decide whether or not

41
00:00:101.630 --> 00:00:102.840
this is right for you.

42
00:00:102.840 --> 00:00:105.410
CPT works just as well either way.

43
00:00:105.410 --> 00:00:107.370
You can also choose to meet with your therapist

44
00:00:107.370 --> 00:00:110.510
one-on-one or in a group with others who have PTSD.

45
00:00:110.510 --> 00:00:114.010
CPT is "evidence-based," meaning it's been shown to work

46
00:00:114.010 --> 00:00:115.850
in multiple research studies.

47
00:00:115.850 --> 00:00:117.620
And it doesn't take years.

48
00:00:117.620 --> 00:00:121.290
In fact, CPT usually takes about three months of weekly visits.

49
00:00:121.290 --> 00:00:125.290
CPT can help you get "unstuck" -- freeing you up to start

50
00:00:125.290 --> 00:00:127.430
enjoying the things you've been missing out on.

51
00:00:127.430 --> 00:00:129.760
Men and women, Veterans and civilians can

52
00:00:129.760 --> 00:00:131.760
all benefit from CPT.

53
00:00:134.130 --> 00:00:136.770
If Cognitive Processing Therapy sounds like it could be right

54
00:00:136.770 --> 00:00:140.440
for you, talk to your doctor about finding a CPT provider.

55
00:00:140.440 --> 00:00:147.110
Or visit the National Center for PTSD website at www.ptsd.va.gov

56
00:00:147.110 --> 00:00:150.750
to learn more about CPT and other PTSD treatments.

57
00:00:150.750 --> 00:00:153.450
No matter how long you've been living with PTSD,

58
00:00:153.450 --> 00:00:155.260
know that you can get better.
